Huh?  In my world, I run a terminal emulator for xterm like this:

xterm -fn "name-of-font-here"

And it starts up.  My X server either finds a font matching that
string, or gets the font server to, in both cases the same result.
Those source fonts could be vector based and scalable, in which case
the server has to scale the font to make a bitmap, or, wait for it, the
font could be available already at that scale, and so the X server is
really just copying bitmaps around.

The only difference is whether or not the X server needs to do an
initial scaling step when the font is requested by the terminal
emulator.  Obviously, xterm uses XDrawText, XDrawString or some other
API call like that.

Where's the apples and orange?  I know I need to give my terminal
emulator some kind of bitmap to write, but I also know that bitmap can
be stored as a bitmap, or can be generated on the fly from vectors.  Is
it really so confusing or strange to consider the tradeoffs between the
two?

